{"query":"America went from 75,000 flu deaths last year in 2019 to almost 0 in 2020 original source fact check","results":[{"position":1,"site_name":"archive.cdc.gov","snippet":"During the 2019-2020 influenza season, CDC estimates that influenza was associated with 38 million illnesses, 18 million medical visits, 405,000 hospitalizations, and 22,000 deaths.","title":"Estimated Influenza-Related Illnesses, Medical Visits ...","url":"https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/flu/about/burden/2019-2020/archive-09292021.html"},{"position":2,"site_name":"archive.cdc.gov","snippet":"This page included links to past flu seasons’ summary reports, where you can find information on the season as well as past reports and publications released.","title":"Past Flu Seasons | CDC","url":"https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/flu/season/past-flu-seasons.htm"},{"position":3,"site_name":"en.wikipedia.org","snippet":"The unusually abrupt decline in cases by April 2020 was attributed to the effects of widespread social distancing and lockdowns aimed at COVID-19, shortening the influenza season by 5–6 weeks.","title":"2019–2020 United States flu season - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019%E2%80%932020_United_States_flu_season"},{"position":4,"site_name":"stacks.cdc.gov","snippet":"During the 2019–2020 season an estimated 38 million people were sick with flu, resulting in 18 million visits to a health care provider, 400,000 hospitalizations, and 22,000 deaths.","title":"Influenza Disease Burden and Vaccine Impact Estimates, 2019 ...","url":"https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/157600/cdc_157600_DS1.pdf"},{"date":"Oct 30, 2020","position":5,"site_name":"www.usatoday.com","snippet":"For the 2019-2020 season, the CDC has released a preliminary estimate of 22,000 influenza related deaths. Even combining those seasons, from late fall 2018 to early spring 2020, there...","title":"Fact check: Donald Trump Jr.'s claim about flu deaths is false","url":"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/fac